{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1: Working with the user profile",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "What you'll do",
      "description": "## What you'll learn\n\n**Here's what you'll do:**\n\n* Explore user profiles in the Auth0 Dashboard.\n* Update the user's user_metadata and app_metadata\n* Retrieve the normalized profile of a user using the Management API."
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a user account",
      "description": "## Create a new user account \n\n1. Log in to your Auth0 Dashboard. If you have multiple tenants, ensure your default **dev-** tenant is selected.\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **User Management** > **Users**.\n1. Select **Create User** > **Create via UI**.\n1. Ensure the **Username-Password-Authentication** database connection is selected. This connection is should be on your tenant by default without you having to create it.\n1. For the email, enter **nina.shah@fake.email**.\n1. Enter a password that you will be able to remember.\n1. Create the user."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Examine user details",
      "description": "## Examine user details\n\n1. After having created the nina.shah@fake.email account, you should be on the user's Details tab.\n\n1. Select the **Actions** button, and examine the options. You can:\n    - Send a verification email\n    - Update the user's email address\n    - Clear the user's trusted MFA devices\n    - Change the user's password\n    - Block the user from signing in\n    - Delete the user from the connection\n\n1. Click out of the Actions menu. Examine the details on the page:\n    - The user's name is set as the email address they registered with\n    - The email has a status of **UNVERIFIED**\n    - The Signed Up date is **today's date**\n    - The Primary Identity Provider is **Database**\n    - Latest Login is **Never**\n\n"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Change user parameters",
      "description": "## Update user attributes\n\nNext, we'll update some of the user attributes.\n\n1. Under the user's **Name**, select the **Edit** link.\n\n1. Update the name to **Nina Shah** and save it.\n\n1. Under the user's **Email**, select **Edit**.\n\n1. Since the user's email is unverified, you will see an option to manually set it to **verified**. Select this link and exit out of the window.\n>You should now see **VERIFIED** next to the user's email address.\n\n1. Scroll down to the **Metadata section**.\n>There are two JSON editor fields in this section: one for **user_metadata** and one for **app_metadata**.\n\n1. Update the **user_metadata** object to include a key of **“favorite_color”** and a string value **\"purple\"**. Your **user_metadata**  editor box should look like:\n\n    ```\n    {\n        \"favorite_color\": \"purple\"\n    }  \n\n\n1. Update the **app_metadata** object to include a key of **“vip”** with a boolean value **true**. Your **app_metadata** editor box should look like:\n\n\n    ```\n    {\n        \"vip\": true\n    }  \n\n\n1. Save your changes.\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 18,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 8
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Prepare to use the Management API Explorer",
      "description": "## Prepare to use the Management API Explorer\n\nNext, we'll retrieve user profiles using the Management API. For this, we'll use the Management API Explorer, which is a great tool to explore and prototype calls to the Management API without having to write a line of code or having use tools like curl or Postman.\n\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **Applications** > **APIs**.\n>The APIs list view is displayed. Notice that an API already exists for your tenant, the **Auth0 Management API**.\n>The Management API is a system API with an identifier of `https://{yourTenantName}.{yourRegion}.auth0.com/api/v2`.\n\n1. Select **Auth0 Management API**.\n>Note the warning alerting you to the fact this is an Auth0 API and cannot be modified or deleted.\n\n1. Select the **Permissions** tab.\\n >These are all the permissions that the Management API is granted.\n\n1. Select the **API Explorer** tab.\n\n1. If you've never used the Management API Explorer before, you will see a **Create & Authorize Test Application** button here. Select that button.\n\n1. Next to the **Token** field, select the **Copy** icon to copy the value.\n>Note that this token includes all the scopes that are granted to the API Explorer Application, so by default it can invoke ALL the Management API endpoints. It should NOT be used on production environments.\n\n1. Review the instructions under the Using this token in the API Explorer section.\n\n1. Select the link listed in the first step of this section to open the Management API Explorer.\n"
    },
    {
      "title": "Retrieve user profiles with the Management API",
      "description": "## Retrieve user profiles using the Management API\n\n1. From the Management API Reference page, locate and select **Users** in the left navigation bar.\n\n1. Select **List or Search Users**.\n> Notice that this is a GET request to `/api/v2/users` and retrieves the details of users. It requires an access token with the following scopes: `read:users` and `read:user_idp_tokens`.\n\n1. Select the **Try it** button.\n\n1. In the **Authorization** field, paste the token value you copied in the previous step next to **Bearer**.\n\n1. In the **tenantDomain** field, you will need to enture the URL of your tenant **without https://**. You can find this at the bottom of this Codespace tab where it says **Auth0: `<your tenant URL>`**.\n\n1. In the **Query** section, you can see all the parameters that are accepted by this endpoint. Notice that they are all optional since they are not marked **required**.\n\n1. Select the **Send** button to issue the request to the Management API.\n\n1. You should see a 200 (Success) response returned with a JSON response containing a list of users registered to your tenant.\n> What if we just wanted to see the user we just created? This might be easy to locate at this point if you don't have many users in your tenant, but what happens when your user base expands? We can use the `q` parameter to include query strings!\n\n1. In the `q` parameter, enter: **name:\"Nina Shah\"** and then select the **Send** button again.\n> Now you can see Nina Shah's profile returned. But what if we have more than one user with the same name? We can also search for individual users by their unique user ID.\n\n1. Scroll through Nina's profile details and locate her **user_id**. \n> There's more than one entry for this -- find the one that starts with **auth0|**.\n\n1. Copy the entire user_id starting with **auth0|**.\n\n1. From the left navigation under Users, select **Get a User**.\n> Notice that this is a GET request that will retrieve a user's detail using their ID. It requires an access token with the following scopes: `read:users` and `read:user_idp_tokens`. The _id_ path parameter is also required.\n\n1. Select the **Try it** button.\n>Your tenantDomain and Authorization values should persist so you don't have to re-enter them.\n\n1. Paste the user ID you copied previously into the **id** field.\n\n1. Select **Send** to send the request.\n> You should get a 200 (Success) response back with Nina Shah's profile details. \n\n1. Look through Nina's profile details again and look for the **user_metadata** and **app_metadata**. You should see the values for **favorite_color** and **vip** you set in the Auth0 Dashboard.\n"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Recap",
      "description": "## What you did\n\nIn this lab you:\n\n* Explored user profiles in the Auth0 Dashboard.\n* Updated the user's user_metadata and app_metadata\n* Retrieved the normalized profile of a user using the Management API.\n"
    }
  ]
}